test: add a layout audit instead of one-off scratch scripts

The checks this session needed were written as seven throwaway scripts in
the temp directory, which meant they were gone the moment I cleaned up. They
are now one tool, in test/verify.py, with the reasoning for each check written
down next to it.

verify.py sweeps 11 viewports in chromium and firefox and exits non-zero on
failure, so it works as a pre-commit gate. --ab re-measures with
index.html, styles.css and app.js swapped for an older git ref and prints the
numbers either side, restoring the files in a finally block. Eyeballing a
fixed page cannot distinguish a real fix from a page that was never broken;
the before/after numbers are the evidence.

The checks are the defects this site actually had: a 61x22 tap target under
the WCAG 2.2 AA floor, commit-meta hidden below 880px so the hash and author
vanished on phones, a legend swatch that did not match the dot it labelled,
a release date breaking mid-string in the 881-1150px band, a nav that was
display:none with no hamburger, and tier-alt blocks staggered by 156px.

Three things worth recording about the tool itself:

- It hash-guards the served styles.css against the file on disk before
  measuring, and refuses to run on a mismatch. A dev server serves from its
  working directory, so it will happily answer 200 from a different checkout
  on the same port and produce a clean-looking result describing somebody
  else's CSS.

- It checks the computed grid track list, not just the visual result, for
  zero-width tracks. A subgrid row-lock mistake collapsed all four pricing
  cards into one band and the grid answered with two implicit zero-width
  columns, and the alignment check still passed in that state, because all
  the cards genuinely were in the same band. Only the track list caught it.

- It builds and runs the server binary directly instead of shelling out to
  `go run`. `go run` execs its result as a child process, so terminating it
  leaves the child holding the port. The first version of this tool leaked a
  server on every run and the next run tripped over it.

The probe is null-safe on elements that do not exist in older revisions, so
--ab against a ref from before .tier-alt was introduced reports "absent"
rather than throwing. The baseline count is reported separately from the gate,
so a failing historical revision does not make a healthy working tree look
broken.

Verified: 22/22 pass on the current tree in both engines; against fa795ce the
baseline correctly fails all 11 viewports and names every original defect;
against e42e53b it shows bandSpread 156 -> 4. No leaked processes, and the
working tree is byte-identical after every --ab run.
